hereditary has been called the scariest  movie of the year and one reason for  that is these creepy model houses like  in this movie it almost the models  almost became a character in the movie  in a sense  Steve Newbern is the owner of Applied  Arts FX studio in Canada he's done  visual effects for the Dark Knight Rises  and inception my team and I we built all  the prosthetics and models for  hereditary if you haven't seen it yet  turn back now because there's some  possible spoilers ahead miniature models  are used all the time in movies filmed  in a way to make them look like a  life-size object usually with miniature  work the intent is not to see it it's  intended to duplicate a real thing but  in hereditary the miniatures were  actually part of the story  Tony Colette's character creates the  models in preparation for a gallery  Steve and his crew were tasked with  making about 15 of them to match the  life-size film sets we had we had a  thousand pieces and at one point and  really didn't know exactly how they went  together the whole process took quite a  bit of time to complete ten weeks with a  crew of about half a dozen people the  buildings included a funeral home a  preschool a hospice and an accident  scene the largest of the models was a  copy of the family home and its  exteriors were modeled after a real  house yeah I went down in 6-8 weeks  before they started filming and just  kind of surveyed the house took you know  several hundred photos a bunch of  measurements that sort of thing he  worked with the director to nail down  all the details flooring they'd sent a  samples wallpaper that would send his  samples all that stuff would be taken  and scaled down to the appropriate scale  the house was built in nine we built the  entire thing as a giant puzzle then  where you can remove sections of it to  shoot from that location the models that  didn't need to resemble the set were  built using generic images online as a  guide like the preschool all 